import Foundation

let pendulumSection = RefSection(
    id: "pendulum", title: "Pendulums", icon: "waveform.path",
    blurb: "The double pendulum, its chaos, the flip-time fractal and how the simulation works. Figures quoted here were measured on this app’s own integrator.",
    groups: [
        RefGroup("The double pendulum", [
            rx("pendulum.double", "Double pendulum", "A pendulum hanging from the end of another pendulum. Two angles θ₁ and θ₂ (measured from straight down) describe it. It has two degrees of freedom and, unlike a single pendulum, can behave chaotically. The app uses two equal point masses on massless rods of equal length, with g = 9.81, no friction and no air drag."),
            rx("pendulum.equations", "Equations of motion", "From the Lagrangian with unit masses and rod lengths. δ = θ₁ − θ₂ and the denominator is 3 − cos 2δ.", "θ̈₁ = [−g(3 sin θ₁ + sin(θ₁ − 2θ₂)) − 2 sin δ (θ̇₂² + θ̇₁² cos δ)] / (3 − cos 2δ)\nθ̈₂ = [2 sin δ (2θ̇₁² + 2g cos θ₁ + θ̇₂² cos δ)] / (3 − cos 2δ)"),
            rx("pendulum.energy", "Energy", "With unit masses and lengths, kinetic energy is T = θ̇₁² + ½θ̇₂² + θ̇₁θ̇₂ cos δ and potential energy is V = −g(2 cos θ₁ + cos θ₂). The total E = T + V is conserved. Hanging at rest is E = −3g ≈ −29.4 J; the app’s Energy control sets E by scaling the starting velocities.", "E = T + V,  V = −g(2cos θ₁ + cos θ₂)"),
            rx("pendulum.threshold", "Flip threshold", "For the outer bob to swing over the top (θ₂ = π) the energy must be at least −g ≈ −9.8 J. Below that the pendulum is locked: it can never flip, no matter how long you wait."),
            rx("pendulum.locked", "Energy-locked region", "Released from rest, a flip is impossible whenever 2 cos θ₁ + cos θ₂ > 1. This proves the central region of the flip-time fractal is dark, with no simulation needed, and gives the ‘Energy-locked’ presets.", "2cos θ₁ + cos θ₂ > 1  ⇒  never flips"),
            rx("pendulum.modes", "Normal modes", "For small angles the motion is a sum of two pure oscillations: a slow mode where both bobs swing together (ω² = g(2 − √2), about 0.38 Hz) and a fast mode where they swing against each other (ω² = g(2 + √2), about 0.92 Hz). Their frequency ratio is 1 + √2 ≈ 2.41.", "θ₂ = ±√2·θ₁"),
            rx("pendulum.quasi", "Quasi-periodic motion", "Motion that is a combination of two incommensurate frequencies. It never exactly repeats but stays on a torus in phase space, so it is regular and predictable. Most small and many large starts behave this way."),
            rx("pendulum.kam", "KAM theory", "The Kolmogorov–Arnold–Moser theorem explains why: for a small enough disturbance most regular tori survive, but chaos fills the gaps between them. As energy rises, the tori are destroyed and chaos spreads; at very high energy the bobs spin and the motion becomes regular again."),
            rx("pendulum.rotor", "Rotor motion", "At high starting energy the bobs spin over the top continuously. The angle then grows without bound, so for sound it is wrapped to ±π, which turns a spinning bob into a sawtooth wave. High-energy starts are regular: a twin started 10⁻⁶ rad away stays glued to the original."),
            rx("pendulum.highenergy", "High-energy presets", "The High energy presets (567 to 2221 J) all stayed regular for 100 simulated seconds, with a 10⁻⁶ twin glued to the original. Lower-energy starts that look like rotors can still be chaotic: ω = (12, −20) has only 75 J and its twin splits away within 7 s."),
        ]),
        RefGroup("Chaos", [
            rx("pendulum.chaos", "Chaos in the double pendulum", "Tiny differences in the starting position grow exponentially, so long-term prediction is impossible even though the equations are exactly deterministic. With the default settings the 10⁻⁶ rad twin separates from the original within roughly 4 to 15 seconds for typical chaotic starts."),
            rx("pendulum.twin", "Chaos twin", "A second pendulum started a tiny angle away (10⁻¹² to 10⁻¹ rad, in θ₁). Plotted on top of the original, it follows it closely at first, then abruptly peels away. The time that takes is the visible face of the Lyapunov exponent."),
            rx("pendulum.lyapunov", "Lyapunov exponent", "The average exponential rate at which two nearby trajectories diverge: separation ≈ ε·e^{λt}. For the chaotic starts measured here λ is roughly 0.5 to 1.5 per second, so an offset of 10⁻⁹ typically grows to 10⁻³ in about 10 seconds."),
            rx("pendulum.horizon", "Predictability horizon", "Because errors grow like e^{λt}, even 64-bit rounding (about 10⁻¹⁶) reaches order 1 after roughly 25 to 70 simulated seconds for a chaotic start. Beyond that, a computed trajectory is one valid-looking path, not the exact answer. Statistics of many trajectories remain meaningful."),
            rx("pendulum.slowchaos", "Slow chaos", "Chaos need not be violent. ‘Slow burn’ looks regular for over a minute before its twin splits (at about 78 s) and it first flips at 153 s. ‘Trapped chaos’ is chaotic yet never flips: it is confined by energy but still unpredictable."),
            rx("pendulum.sensitive", "Sensitive dependence", "The defining property of chaos. In the flip-time fractal it shows up as infinitely fine structure: no matter how far you zoom, neighbouring starts have very different fates."),
        ]),
        RefGroup("The flip-time fractal", [
            rx("pendulum.fractal", "Flip-time fractal", "Every pixel is a pair of starting angles (θ₁ across, θ₂ up), released from rest. The colour shows how long until either bob first flips over the top (|θ| exceeds π); dark means it never flips in the simulated time. The boundary between flipping and non-flipping regions is fractal."),
            rx("pendulum.flip", "What counts as a flip", "A bob has flipped when its angle, measured as total rotation from straight down, first exceeds π in magnitude: it has gone over the top."),
            rx("pendulum.islands", "Islands of stability", "Dark regions inside the chaotic sea where the pendulum never flips and its motion is regular. Verified examples: the Great island near (θ₁, θ₂) = (85°, 68°), which stayed regular for 1000 simulated seconds, and a smaller Second island near (49.5°, 98°). Many apparent islands in short runs turned out to be slow chaos that flipped after 73 to 214 seconds, so longer simulated times shrink the island list."),
            rx("pendulum.tiling", "Periodic tiling", "Angles repeat every 2π, so the picture repeats forever in both directions. Panning continues across tiles with no seam: the same starting position simply has several equivalent coordinates."),
            rx("pendulum.grow", "Grow mode", "Every pixel’s pendulum is released at once and a pixel lights up in its flip-time colour when its pendulum flips. Watching time pass reveals the structure being generated. When the clock reaches the simulated time, the picture equals the static flip map."),
            rx("pendulum.live", "Live mode", "Every pixel is a live pendulum, coloured by its current angles (hue from θ₁, brightness from θ₂). Neighbouring pixels start together and drift apart as the chaos amplifies their differences."),
            rx("pendulum.quality", "Quality, step size and accuracy", "The picture is computed with RK4 at step 0.02 (Fast), 0.01 (Normal) or 0.005 (Fine). Against a 0.001 reference at 10 s, the Fast step misclassifies about 0.5% of pixels, Normal about 0.09% and Fine about 0.007%, almost all along chaotic boundaries."),
        ]),
        RefGroup("Simulation & sound", [
            rx("pendulum.rk4", "RK4 integrator", "The classic fourth-order Runge–Kutta method evaluates the derivative four times per step and combines them, giving an error that shrinks like (step)⁴. The live simulation shrinks the step further for fast-spinning bobs.", "y ← y + (dt/6)(k₁ + 2k₂ + 2k₃ + k₄)"),
            rx("pendulum.reliability", "Reliability", "The acceleration formula agrees with an independent mass-matrix derivation to 7 × 10⁻¹³. Energy is conserved to about 6 × 10⁻⁵ J over 100 simulated seconds at step 0.004 for a chaotic start, and to about 10⁻⁷ for regular ones. A step of 0.02 loses about 0.09 J over 100 s, which is why the app uses smaller steps for live motion."),
            rx("pendulum.symplectic", "Energy drift", "RK4 is not symplectic, so energy drifts slowly instead of oscillating around the true value; the drift shown by the Three-Body tool is the same effect."),
            rx("pendulum.model", "Model limits", "Real double pendulums have friction, air drag, bearing play and unequal, extended masses (compound pendulums). This simulation is the idealised version, so a real one stops being chaotic-looking as friction drains its energy."),
            rx("pendulum.sound", "Listening to the pendulum", "θ₁(t) becomes the left speaker’s waveform and θ₂(t) the right’s. The pendulum is simulated at audio rate, 2⁵ to 2¹¹ simulated seconds per real second, so its slow swinging lands in the audible range. Angles are wrapped to ±π and high-pass filtered, and a slow automatic gain keeps quiet motions audible."),
            rx("pendulum.pitch", "Pitch setting", "The time scale multiplies every frequency. The slow normal mode (0.38 Hz) at 256× sounds near 98 Hz (about G2); the fast mode (0.92 Hz) near 235 Hz."),
            rx("pendulum.timbre", "Timbre of the motion", "Pure modes are sine waves. Large regular orbits add harmonics (the Great island has strong 3rd and 5th harmonics, a hollow clarinet-like tone). Spinning bobs give sawtooth waves. Chaotic motion sounds like a rough, wandering band of noise."),
            rx("pendulum.presets", "Presets", "Presets are grouped as Coherent, High energy, Energy-locked, Islands of stability, Neat sounds, Chaotic and Slow chaos. Each one shows a note with timings measured on this simulator: first flip, twin separation and verified durations."),
        ]),
    ])
